@page "/agents"

<PageTitle>Ask it with an agent · Linq2Dashboard</PageTitle>

<h1>Ask it with an agent</h1>

<p>
    <code>Linq2Dashboard.AI</code> lets an AI agent answer questions about a dashboard's rows: "which country had the most revenue
    this year", "the five largest open orders", "show me the cancelled orders in Norway". It turns one dashboard into three tools,
    describe, query and show, as <code>Microsoft.Extensions.AI</code> functions. Agent Framework, Semantic Kernel, a plain
    <code>IChatClient</code> and the MCP C# SDK all take them. The answers come from the same facets and metrics the screen uses, so
    the agent's numbers agree with the counts the user sees.
</p>

<p>This page continues the <a href="cached-dashboard">cached dashboard</a> app. At the bottom you can run the tools yourself, in your browser.</p>

<h2>1. The packages</h2>

<p>The AI package, and <code>Microsoft.Extensions.AI</code> for the chat client that calls the tools. Add your model provider's package as well.</p>

<pre><code>@PackageExample</code></pre>

<h2>2. The tools</h2>

<p>
    Make the tools from the dashboard the page already uses. <code>Name</code> prefixes the tool names, <code>Summary</code> tells the agent
    what the rows are, and <code>Allowed</code> is the list of facets, metrics and properties the agent may use. The descriptions matter
    most: the agent cannot know what a status means or what currency an amount is in unless you tell it.
</p>

<pre><code>@ToolsExample</code></pre>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>orders_describe</code></td><td>Lists what can be asked: facets with their values when there are few, metrics, properties, date periods, limits, and the fields a where can filter on. The agent calls it once, first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>orders_query</code></td><td>Asks one question and returns one table. One parameter per part: <code>selections</code>, <code>where</code>, <code>groupBy</code>, <code>values</code>, <code>columns</code>, <code>orderBy</code>, <code>take</code>. Reads only; nothing changes on screen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>orders_show</code></td><td>Puts selections on the user's dashboard. Only there when you give <code>Show</code>, below.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2>3. Give them to a chat client</h2>

<p>
    Function invocation runs the loop: the model asks for a tool, the client calls it and hands the result back, until the model
    answers. A question usually takes two or three calls.
</p>

<pre><code>@ChatExample</code></pre>

<h2>4. Show the answer on screen</h2>

<p>
    With <code>Show</code>, the agent can also filter the user's dashboard, for "show me" rather than "tell me". Bind the view's selections
    and set them from the callback. The selections are checked before your callback runs, so a facet the agent made up never reaches the page.
</p>

<pre><code>@ShowExample</code></pre>

<h2>What the agent needs from you</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Descriptions</strong> for every name whose value is not self-explanatory. An id with a label above all: without a description, the agent gives the user the number when they asked for the name.</li>
    <li><strong>Allowed</strong> keeps internal fields away from the agent. It limits names, not rows: the agent sees every row of the dashboard you give it. A user who may see only some rows gets tools over their own scope, <code>dashboard.ScopeTo(...)</code>, as their screen does.</li>
    <li><strong>One <code>Name</code> per dashboard</strong> when an app has several, so the agent picks <code>orders_query</code> or <code>tickets_query</code> by name.</li>
    <li><strong>Nothing else.</strong> The tools cache nothing, hold no conversation and write nothing. Errors in what the agent asked come back to it as results with the known names, and it corrects itself.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Try the tools</h2>

<p>
    The panel calls the query tool exactly as an agent would, over @(Rows.ToString("N0", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))
    generated orders built in your browser (the <a href="demo">demo</a>'s data). Pick an example or write your own; the where is
    Gridify syntax, such as <code>Amount&gt;=1000,Channel=EDI|Portal</code>.
</p>

@if (tools is null)
{
    <p class="agents-muted">Building the dashboard...</p>
}
else
{
    <div class="agents-examples">
        @foreach (Example example in Examples)
        {
            <button type="button" class="agents-example" @onclick="() => Load(example)">@example.Title</button>
        }
        <button type="button" class="agents-example" @onclick="RunDescribe">Describe</button>
    </div>

    <form class="agents-form" @onsubmit="RunQuery">
        <label>selections <input @bind="selectionsText" placeholder="Status=Shipped&OrderDate=thisYear" /></label>
        <label>where <input @bind="whereText" placeholder="Amount>=1000" /></label>
        <label>groupBy <input @bind="groupByText" placeholder="Country, OrderDate:Quarter" /></label>
        <label>values <input @bind="valuesText" placeholder="revenue, orders, avg:Amount" /></label>
        <label>columns <input @bind="columnsText" placeholder="Id, Customer, Amount" /></label>
        <label>orderBy <input @bind="orderByText" placeholder="-revenue" /></label>
        <label>take <input @bind="takeText" placeholder="10" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
        <button type="submit" class="agents-run">Run orders_query</button>
    </form>

    @if (call is not null)
    {
        <p class="agents-muted">The call, as the agent sends it:</p>
        <pre><code>@call</code></pre>
        <p class="agents-muted">What it gets back, run @runs@(elapsed is null ? null : $", in {elapsed} ms"):</p>
        <pre class="agents-result"><code>@result</code></pre>
    }
}
